@@ -0,0 +1,337 @@
+# Issue 31 invitation transport boundary
+
+**Architecture review: the unimplemented raw-token outbox, five-RPC delivery interface,
+and recipient-continuation sections below are paused proposals under replacement review.
+They are not an authorized database implementation packet.** The current direction is
+exactly-once database acceptance, a supported password flow, and nonsecret delivery
+status. Preserve the existing source for review; do not implement the proposed outbox or
+five RPCs from this document.
+
+**Completion status: source-only and incomplete.** The repository source defines a
+server-only Resend HTTPS adapter, preserves the browser's invitation operation identity,
+and defines a durable delivery reconciliation seam. It does not make issue 31 deployable
+by itself. A reviewed replacement architecture, its integrated database and callback-safety
+changes, provider and hosted configuration, deployment, and a human-observed staging
+delivery all remain required. This source work performs no provider-account, payment, DNS, hosted
+Supabase, secret, deployment, public-routing, or live-email action.
+
+## Source checkpoint and pinned database comparison
+
+The 2026-09-04 source checkpoint is based on mail commit
+`e1abd80770b19bb2369f8ce4502408c4f994f7bf` plus its uncommitted transport changes.
+Local verification used Node.js 24.20.0, npm 11.12.1, and the existing dependency
+overlay: all 99 Edge tests and all 310 focused web/controller/adapter/port/contracts
+tests passed. Scoped ESLint, Deno lint, Deno check, and web/contracts TypeScript
+checks passed. These tests inject the database and provider boundaries; they do
+not prove a real Resend send, PostgREST integration, or recipient reload recovery.
+
+Read-only comparison against the clean password-receipts database commit
+`555e5820eb041e9c63272fc6413ce084a77f4f74` confirms that the database gates below
+remain open. In `supabase/migrations/20260903000100_staff_auth_lifecycle.sql`:
+
+- None of the five delivery RPCs specified below exists. The older
+  `internal_prepare_staff_invitation`, `internal_publish_staff_invitation`, and
+  `internal_fail_staff_invitation` return only `code` and do not carry a durable
+  delivery-attempt identity or provider acceptance receipt. Renaming the Edge
+  calls cannot supply the missing semantics.
+- The initial `begin_staff_invitation` arguments and two-column response match,
+  but exact replay returns the original invitation only in `pending_delivery`.
+  Replay after preparation, publication, or acceptance returns `conflict`, so the
+  sender cannot yet reach the new reconciliation path after those boundaries.
+- `current_staff_invitations` projects only `pending_acceptance`; there is no
+  publication-pending observation or durable mail-provenance continuation.
+  Password receipts begin after publication and do not recover that earlier race.
+- New delivery timestamps need an explicit wire contract: this transport requires
+  the exact JavaScript ISO serialization with milliseconds and `Z`, including an
+  exact match between the top-level timestamp and frozen attempt. The older
+  contract's normalization of timestamp offsets or extra fractional digits must
+  not silently alter persisted provider bytes.
+
+The source-only checkpoint is therefore ready for independent source review,
+while issue 31 remains incomplete. Implement and test the coordinated database
+and callback contracts before treating this branch as integrated.
+
+The transport sends only through `POST https://api.resend.com/emails`, matching Resend's
+[HTTPS API and send-email contract](https://resend.com/docs/api-reference/emails/send-email).
+The endpoint is a source constant, redirects are rejected, and the runtime must perform normal HTTPS
+certificate and hostname verification for exactly `api.resend.com`. There is no
+configurable provider origin, path, downgrade, proxy, or alternate delivery route. The
+request deadline is eight seconds and the response body limit is 16,384 bytes. Only a
+2xx response with JSON content, the exact response shape `{ id }`, and a syntactically
+valid canonical provider message identifier is acceptance. Network failures, timeouts,
+body-read failures, oversized or malformed responses, and malformed 2xx responses are
+ambiguous and fail closed. A response must never be treated as accepted merely because
+the provider may have received the request.
+
+## Callback contract
+
+The email contains one direct, absolute HTTPS link to the configured web origin's root
+path, serialized exactly as
+`https://<configured-origin>/?auth=invite#token_hash=<56-lowercase-hex-characters>`.
+The query contains only the discriminator `auth=invite`; the fragment contains only
+`token_hash` with exactly 56 lowercase hexadecimal characters. The bearer never appears
+in the HTTP request path or query. Legacy query tokens, mixed query/fragment credentials,
+extra or duplicate fields, percent-encoded fragment keys or values, and other
+noncanonical serializations fail closed. There is no URL user information, alternate
+origin or path, redirector, or tracking link. The URL carries no invitation ID,
+browser operation ID, delivery-attempt ID, PKCE verifier, or PKCE challenge.
+PKCE does not participate in this invitation exchange; the browser neither supplies nor
+derives a verifier or challenge for the emailed link.
+
+Despite its name, the `token_hash` value is the one-time bearer credential. It is not an
+identifier or safe telemetry. The browser must synchronously capture and scrub all
+callback material, hold the credential only in memory, render a non-consuming landing,
+and invoke provider verification only after an explicit human confirmation. A GET,
+preview, prefetch, crawler, or JavaScript-capable mail scanner must not consume the
+credential. The email copy explicitly directs the recipient to open the link and then
+confirm in the browser; it introduces no alternate consumption path.
+
+This lane owns construction and validation of that direct link and the controlled
+plain-text invitation copy. The callback-safety web lane owns synchronous scrubbing,
+the explicit confirmation, memory-only credential handling, and prevention of startup
+auto-consumption. No Supabase verification or recovery email-template change is part of
+this transport: the Edge function uses the server-side generated invite material and
+sends its own controlled message. Any hosted redirect allowlist, authentication expiry,
+verification-template, or recovery-template review is a later human-owned configuration
+gate. Recovery behavior must remain unchanged unless separately reviewed and approved.
+
+## Browser operation identity
+
+The browser creates one canonical UUID `operationId` for an invitation submission. The
+contracts, adapter, port, and Edge request carry that value unchanged. The Edge function
+passes it unchanged as `request_key` to `begin_staff_invitation`; it must not generate a
+replacement request key. A browser retry retains the original mutation and initiating
+actor authority and reuses the same operation ID. It does not rebuild the request from
+current form state or allocate new entropy. The controller and adapter pin the initiating
+session and identity with in-memory expected-generation fields before egress; those
+fields are never forwarded over the network. Matching authentication events discard the
+retained retry, and pending provider authentication events block invocation until the
+authority check can settle.
+
+The database must bind the exact owner actor, workspace, normalized recipient email,
+role, operation/request key, and invitation ID. An exact replay returns the same
+invitation ID through every delivery phase and after publication; any changed field with
+the same key returns `conflict`. This replay invariant is what allows the Edge function
+to reconcile durable delivery state. Passing the operation ID through without extending
+database replay beyond `pending_delivery` is insufficient.
+
+The operation ID and request key are nonsecret correlation values, but they are still
+excluded from logs and public projections. No request body, recipient, callback, bearer
+credential, exact provider payload, authorization value, provider credential, or
+provider response detail may be logged or included in a public error.
+
+## Frozen Resend attempt
+
+One invitation operation has one durable delivery-attempt identity. Before any provider
+egress, the service freezes and atomically persists these exact service-only fields:
+
+- `attemptId`, `attemptStartedAt`, `invitationId`, and `recipientEmail`;
+- `callbackUrl` and `expiresAt`;
+- `copyVersion`, `from`, and `replyTo`;
+- `idempotencyKey`; and
+- `providerRequestBody`, the exact serialized bytes sent to Resend.
+
+The configured values are strict and server-only: `RESEND_API_KEY`,
+`DESMOSLOCK_STAFF_INVITATION_FROM`, and
+`DESMOSLOCK_STAFF_INVITATION_REPLY_TO`. The From value is one canonical mailbox or one
+strict display-name/mailbox form; Reply-To is one canonical mailbox. The configured
+callback origin must exactly match the HTTPS web callback origin. Missing, malformed,
+extra, or drifting configuration fails closed.
+
+The frozen provider body has exactly `from`, `to`, `reply_to`, `subject`, and `text`.
+`to` is a one-element array containing the exact canonical recipient. The subject and
+plain-text copy are source controlled by `staff-invitation-v1`. There is no HTML body,
+dynamic template, attachment, custom header, tracking parameter, or per-message tracking
+field. Retrying recomputes and verifies the entire frozen attempt, then sends the exact
+same body bytes with the exact same configured From, Reply-To, copy version, and callback.
+Configuration drift is not repaired by mutating a pending attempt.
+
+The nonsecret Resend idempotency key is deterministically derived only from the
+invitation ID and delivery-attempt ID. It contains neither recipient nor callback
+material and remains within Resend's 1–256 character limit. Resend's
+[idempotency contract](https://resend.com/docs/dashboard/emails/idempotency-keys)
+retains results for 24 hours, so automatic source retries stop at 23 hours after
+`attemptStartedAt`, and never run at or after invitation expiry. Any database observation
+or transition lease must be chosen by the database lane and remain comfortably below
+Resend's 24-hour retention window; it must not extend the source's 23-hour automatic
+retry cutoff. Expiry of such a lease must never grant a second Auth-materialization
+claim. Once a one-time invite may have been generated, recovery is reconciliation or
+explicit human handling, not replacement material.
+
+Resend [click and open tracking](https://resend.com/docs/dashboard/domains/tracking)
+are domain settings; the send-email request has no
+per-message tracking-disable field. Source therefore does not invent one. It avoids
+tracking-specific links and headers, while a human must verify the exact sending domain
+has `click_tracking=false` before deployment and should also verify
+`open_tracking=false`. That check is a release prerequisite because a later domain-level
+setting change could otherwise violate the direct-link boundary.
+
+## Schema-neutral database interface
+
+This source intentionally does not prescribe tables or columns. The database lane may
+choose its storage design, provided it implements the following five service-role-only
+RPC names and semantics. All arguments are exact comparison-and-swap inputs; unexpected
+or mismatched values conflict rather than being normalized into a new attempt.
+
+| RPC                                                    | Exact request fields                                                               |
+| ------------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `internal_reconcile_staff_invitation_delivery`         | `invitation_id`, `request_key`, `claim_key`                                        |
+| `internal_prepare_staff_invitation_delivery`           | `invitation_id`, `request_key`, `claim_key`, `invited_user_id`, `delivery_attempt` |
+| `internal_record_staff_invitation_delivery_acceptance` | `invitation_id`, `request_key`, `attempt_id`, `provider_message_id`                |
+| `internal_publish_staff_invitation_delivery`           | `invitation_id`, `request_key`, `attempt_id`, `provider_message_id`                |
+| `internal_fail_staff_invitation_materialization`       | `invitation_id`, `request_key`, `claim_key`, `attempt_id`, `reason_code`           |
+
+Every successful RPC response is exactly one row with exactly these keys:
+`code`, `attempt_id`, `attempt_started_at`, `delivery_attempt`, and
+`provider_message_id`. No additional diagnostic or confidential field is permitted. The
+Edge parser accepts these combinations only:
+
+| `code`                                                                                                 | Required detail                                                                                                                   |
+| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `invitation_delivery_claimed`                                                                          | Canonical `attempt_id` and `attempt_started_at`; the other two detail fields are null.                                            |
+| `invitation_delivery_in_progress`                                                                      | All four detail fields are null.                                                                                                  |
+| `invitation_delivery_ready`                                                                            | Canonical `attempt_id` and `attempt_started_at` exactly matching the persisted `delivery_attempt`; `provider_message_id` is null. |
+| `invitation_provider_accepted`                                                                         | Canonical `attempt_id` and provider message ID; `attempt_started_at` and `delivery_attempt` are null.                             |
+| `invitation_usable`                                                                                    | The exact accepted `attempt_id` and provider message ID; `attempt_started_at` and `delivery_attempt` are null.                    |
+| `conflict`                                                                                             | All four detail fields are null.                                                                                                  |
+| `existing_confirmed_user_unsupported`, `invitation_expired`, `invitation_not_found`, or `rate_limited` | All four detail fields are null.                                                                                                  |
+
+HTTP failure, a non-singleton result, extra or missing keys, an unknown code, malformed
+timestamps or identifiers, a malformed frozen attempt, or an inconsistent receipt maps
+to `dependency_unavailable`. It never authorizes generation, delivery, acceptance, or
+publication.
+
+`internal_reconcile_staff_invitation_delivery` grants
+`invitation_delivery_claimed` to only one exact invocation-scoped `claim_key`. Only that
+state authorizes one call to generate Auth invite material. Another invocation sees
+`invitation_delivery_in_progress`, a frozen ready attempt, an accepted receipt, a
+published result, or a terminal result; it may never generate replacement Auth material.
+Preparation atomically binds the generated Auth user and immutable delivery attempt to
+the invitation. Exact preparation replay returns ready, while a changed user, claim,
+attempt identity, callback, body byte, or payload-affecting field conflicts.
+
+`internal_record_staff_invitation_delivery_acceptance` is the durable commit point for
+provider acceptance. It compare-and-swaps the exact invitation, request key, attempt ID,
+and syntactically valid provider message ID. Exact replay returns accepted (or already
+published); a different attempt or message ID conflicts. This receipt must commit before
+publication. Failure cleanup may terminalize only a still-owned materialization that has
+not persisted a ready attempt or receipt. It must never erase, replace, or retract ready,
+provider-accepted, published, or recipient-continuation state.
+
+`internal_publish_staff_invitation_delivery` requires the exact recorded attempt and
+provider message receipt. It atomically exposes the invitation for recipient acceptance.
+Exact replay returns `invitation_usable`, including after the recipient has already
+accepted, so a lost publication response cannot turn successful delivery into a conflict.
+The attempt identity and receipt therefore outlive public invitation-status transitions
+and ordinary cleanup. They remain service-only and absent from management, recipient,
+and audit projections.
+
+## Retry and reconciliation matrix
+
+| Durable observation or boundary result                                                | Required action                                                                                                                                                   |
+| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| No attempt, exact claim granted                                                       | Generate Auth invite material once, freeze the attempt, and atomically prepare it before egress.                                                                  |
+| Materialization in progress                                                           | Return service unavailable. Do not generate, send, or publish. A lease may enable diagnosis, never a second generation claim.                                     |
+| Ready, within the 23-hour and invitation-expiry bounds                                | Send only the persisted body and deterministic key. Do not regenerate Auth material.                                                                              |
+| Provider returns 2xx plus a valid message ID                                          | Persist the exact acceptance receipt, then publish with that receipt.                                                                                             |
+| Provider accepts but its response is lost                                             | Preserve ready. A later call retries the identical body and key within the retention bound, allowing provider idempotency to reconcile one logical email.         |
+| Network, timeout, or ambiguous/malformed response                                     | Preserve ready and return service unavailable. Retry only the exact attempt within its bounds.                                                                    |
+| Concurrent-idempotency conflict                                                       | Treat as ambiguous, preserve ready, and retry the exact attempt later; do not rotate the key.                                                                     |
+| Same key with a provider-reported different payload                                   | Treat as invariant drift and fail closed. Do not alter the body or allocate another key.                                                                          |
+| Any other non-2xx response                                                            | Do not accept or publish. Preserve the frozen attempt and return service unavailable; any later policy-authorized retry must remain exact and within both bounds. |
+| Acceptance-write response is lost                                                     | Reconcile first. Accepted or published skips Resend; still-ready may repeat only the identical idempotent send within the bound.                                  |
+| Provider acceptance is recorded                                                       | Skip Auth generation and Resend; publish only the exact receipt.                                                                                                  |
+| Publication response is lost                                                          | Reconcile. Published is success; accepted retries publication only.                                                                                               |
+| Already published, including recipient already accepted                               | Return the original success without Auth generation, Resend, or replacement material.                                                                             |
+| Expired, terminal, conflicting, malformed, or past the 23-hour automatic retry cutoff | Fail closed with no provider request and no replacement link. Preserve evidence for human reconciliation where policy requires it.                                |
+
+No branch sends after a recorded provider acceptance. No ambiguous branch creates a new
+invitation, request key, attempt ID, Auth link, callback credential, Resend idempotency
+key, or payload.
+
+## Recipient continuation before publication
+
+Provider delivery and invitation publication are separate commits. A recipient may
+explicitly consume the one-time credential after delivery while the sender's publication
+call is paused or ambiguous. That exact verified recipient must not be stranded, and the
+system must not solve the race by verifying the one-time credential again.
+
+The current database returns `invitation_not_found` for `delivery_ready`, exactly as it
+does for a missing or mismatched invitation, and mints no continuation receipt. That
+behavior is insufficient for durable recovery. The required DB/web contract adds a new
+exact `invitation_publication_pending` observation only when the authenticated user ID
+and canonical email match the live mail-provenance record for the prepared or
+provider-accepted invitation. The result remains detail-free: `invitation_id`,
+`school_id`, `school_name`, `invited_role`, and `expires_at` are all null. Wrong user,
+wrong email, wrong session binding, missing provenance, expiry, revocation, and terminal
+state still return the existing generic `invitation_not_found` result.
+
+Before the one-time credential is discarded, the callback lane and DB must establish a
+durable opaque, non-bearer continuation that binds the exact mail provenance,
+invitation, invited Auth user, and Auth session. The opaque continuation is not placed in
+the email URL and grants neither verification nor invitation acceptance. The reference
+transition contract in
+`supabase/functions/staff-invitations/recipient-publication-continuation.ts` specifies
+the required decision boundary without defining a route, table, or migration. A wrong
+user/session probe receives only `not_found` and cannot erase the rightful unexpired
+continuation; expiry or a terminal invitation retires it.
+
+The web adapter maps only the new exact pending observation to an interrupted inspection
+state, retains or rehydrates the continuation bound to the same account and Auth session,
+and never retains or reconstructs the consumed bearer credential. Same-session refresh
+and reload resume from the opaque continuation without provider verification. A changed
+account or session cannot use it. The concrete provenance exchange, persistence, and
+rehydration route/RPC remain a joint DB/web/mail design and implementation gate; this
+source-only branch intentionally does not guess their storage or deploy an unwired
+public endpoint.
+
+After the sender reconciles the recorded provider acceptance and publishes, the same
+recipient RPC returns the existing full `ok` invitation to that same session. The browser
+resumes the in-memory continuation, presents the invitation, and can perform normal
+acceptance. A focused cross-layer proof must pause publication, perform one explicit
+human-confirmed provider verification, observe generic publication pending, publish the
+exact receipt later, resume the same session without the credential, and accept. It must
+also prove that the provider-verification count remains one and that a wrong session
+reveals nothing and fails closed.
+
+The generic OTP authentication-method reference does not durably establish which
+invitation email was verified, so it cannot substitute for the required mail-provenance
+binding. The pure reference state-machine tests prove the opaque binding decisions, but
+the actual route/RPC, persistence, browser rehydration, and cross-layer proof remain
+integration dependencies. Until they are implemented and tested, a reload in the pause
+window can still strand an otherwise verified recipient and the P1 acceptance criterion
+is not complete.
+
+## Integration and deployment order
+
+Source integration must keep the contracts, web operation-ID forwarding and exact retry,
+Edge transport/orchestrator, database RPC patch, and callback-safety/recipient-
+continuation changes on reviewable commits. The database lane owns migrations and pgTAP;
+this transport lane owns no schema edit. Integrate and verify the shared source contract
+first, then the database implementation against these exact RPC names and shapes, then
+the web callback-safety behavior and durable same-session continuation implementation.
+Resolve and review the concrete mail-provenance route/RPC jointly rather than adding an
+uncoordinated transport-owned endpoint. Run all contract, web, Edge, database,
+static-security, formatting, lint, type, and repository verification checks on the
+combined commit before any hosted action.
+
+Deployment must be a coordinated release because the required browser request and exact
+Edge parser are not mutually compatible with either old side. After approval, deploy the
+backward-compatible database migration/RPCs first. A human then verifies hosted Auth
+redirect and expiry settings, the exact Resend sending domain and sender, disabled click
+tracking (and preferably disabled open tracking), strict From/Reply-To values, and
+server-only secret placement. Deploy the Edge function and callback-safe web artifact as
+one controlled release window; do not expose the new Edge contract to the old web client
+or vice versa. No client bundle may contain the provider credential.
+
+The remaining release gates are database implementation and recovery tests; web callback
+scrubbing, explicit-human confirmation, and same-session publication-pending recovery;
+joint DB/web/mail provenance for durable reload and changed-session recovery;
+combined local and CI verification; provider account approval if needed; DNS and sender
+verification; hosted Supabase redirect, invite-expiry, and template/recovery review;
+server-only credential configuration; deployment authorization; and one authorized
+staging email observed end to end. That staging receipt must prove a non-consuming GET,
+one explicit-human credential exchange, the paused-publication continuation sequence,
+exact sender/copy/link behavior, one logical email across a forced ambiguous retry, no
+tracking rewrite, and successful same-session acceptance. Until every gate is recorded,
+issue 31 remains incomplete.
